@@ -122,3 +122,25 @@ cp .env.example .env
 | `PORT` | no | `7070` | Port to listen on |
 | `ACCESS_TOKEN` | no | _(none)_ | Enforce a specific bearer token; omit to disable auth |
 | `SEED_DIR` | no | `./seed` | Directory of project seed folders to load on boot |
+
+## Migration history
+
+The HTTP migration routes and Postgres-wire CLI workflows share each project's
+`supabase_migrations.schema_migrations` table. HTTP-applied SQL is recorded in
+`statements`, with optional `name` and `rollback` metadata; rollback SQL is not
+executed when applying a migration. Lists read the current table in version
+order, including CLI pushes and repairs.
+
+HTTP migrations apply their SQL and history row in one PGlite transaction.
+Concurrent HTTP migrations are serialized and allocate unused timestamp-shaped
+versions, including when the clock has not advanced or the CLI already used a
+version. Submitted SQL must not manage its own transaction (including savepoints).
+SQL is parsed and executed with `standard_conforming_strings` enabled.
+This keeps SQL changes and their history atomic. The project remains in-memory;
+sharing the database ledger does not add persistence across platform restarts.
+
+PGlite has one database session per project. As with the existing wire server,
+finish CLI/wire work before using the HTTP API on that project: overlapping wire
+transactions and HTTP requests are not supported. This does not add
+`Idempotency-Key` replay behavior; retrying an HTTP migration can execute SQL
+again. The existing HTTP 201 response with `{ version, name? }` is retained.
